  "song": "chunk-v2-s-astamo-close-jnanadhikara",
  "verse": 33,
  "plain_english": "**The knowers are one, and the one is the Lord**\n\nThe jñānādhikāra — the whole first adhikāra of the Īśvarapratyabhijñāvivṛtivimarśinī — comes to its\nclose. And it closes, fittingly, on the unity. It was said at the start that the one is the maheśvara;\nnow the unity is established. Kārikā 10: without that unity, the world-path of the consciousnesses\nwould not be; from the manifestation-unity, that one-ness, that one alone is the knower, it is\nestablished.\n\nThe argument is simple and decisive. The whole dealing of the world — the path by which consciousnesses\nknow, remember, and act together — would be impossible if the consciousnesses were a scattered crowd of\nmutually opaque streams. But the dealing is; so the consciousnesses must be one. And this is not hard to\naccept, for the manifestation (prakāśa) is the very essence of the consciousnesses, and the\nmanifestation is one — there is no difference in the light itself. That one manifestation is, in truth,\nthe knower. The one light that shines in all the consciousnesses is the one knower, the param-essence\nknower, the paramātman. Even the Sāṃkhya's and the Jaiminīya's self-luminous consciousness, and the\nancient Vaiśeṣika's consciousness-nature self, all point to this one.\n\nThe objection comes: \"each cognition rests, in its own self-experience, in its own joining — what is\nnew here?\" And the answer is that the known itself — the blue and the rest — has the manifestation as\nits param-essence; the blue is, in its being, the light. So the unity is real, not a construction. And\nthe apparent difference of the known is made only by the māyā-power: the known-marker part, made\ndistinct from the manifestation as if it were other, is still the manifestation's own part. The\ndifference is the māyā's making; the unity is the truth.\n\nAnd the Sāṃkhya's puruṣa — the separate self it posits — is not another at all. It is the un-recognized\nown-lordship, the contracted consciousness; its knowledge and action, dealt as \"I,\" are the\n\"I\"-recollection. And this \"I\"-recollection, Abhinavagupta notes, is the pervader of all the states —\nthe pure, the mixed, the un-pure — gathered, not abandoned. The threefold self-recognition runs through\nthem all.\n\nThen kārikā 11 sums up the whole: he alone, by the fixed reflexive-awareness-ness, is the great Lord;\nthe reflexive-awareness alone, of the God, in the pure knowledge-action, since. The great-Lordship is\nnothing but the reflexive-awareness — the one who is aware of himself, fixedly, is the Lord; and that\nreflexive-awareness is the God's own pure knowledge-and-action. The great-Lordship is not an added\npower; it is the self-awareness itself, the \"I\" that knows and acts, the one knower.\n\nAnd so the jñānādhikāra — the adhikāra on knowledge — is complete. The ||8|| colophon closes the\nvimarśa on the great-Lordship in the one-support of the powers; and the great colophon reads:\n\"completed — this, the first jñānādhikāra.\" The first of the four adhikāras of the IPVV is done. It\nhas established the self, the powers, the one knower, the great-Lordship — all culminating in the\nunity: the one manifestation is the one knower, and the one knower, aware of himself, is the Lord.\n\n---\n",
  "sanskrit_cite": "tadaikyena vinā na syātsaṃvidāṃ lokapaddhatiḥ | prakāśaikyāt tadekatvaṃ mātaikaḥ sa iti sthitam | sa eva vimṛśattvena niyatena maheśvaraḥ | samāptaśca ayaṃ jñānādhikāraḥ",
